Subject: register VM: tail calls on the record path

What this does:
  tail_call/tail_call_method used to do a nested JS_CallInternal in their
  threaded handlers. They now classify the callee: for a record-eligible
  bytecode function whose caller is itself a nested record, the current frame
  is released and the callee is entered as a sibling record that returns
  straight to the current frame's caller, so a tail-recursive loop grows
  neither the C stack nor the frame arena. Everything the callee needs from
  the old frame (the argument values, the receiver for the method form, and
  the callee object) is duplicated before the release. Natives, bound/proxy
  callees, generator frames, and tail calls from the activation entry record
  fall back to the original nested call.

Why we need it:
  richards executes 7.9M tail_call_method, deltablue 7.06M, typescript 2.1M
  (per-row ##ROP## histograms). Each was a full nested call that could not be
  returned through; frame reuse makes the tail-call rows cheaper and removes
  their C-stack growth.

Needs a bytecode version bump:
  No. Runtime only.

Notes:
  The tail-chain cap (REGVM_TAIL_CHAIN_MAX, 1<<20) bounds consecutive frame
  reuse so runaway tail recursion still raises RangeError like the stack VM
  (the differential corpus's stack-overflow kernel). The counter lives on
  JSRuntime, not in the frame: growing sizeof(JSRegRun) by 8 bytes was
  measured to cost ~8% cycles on navierstokes. regvm_record_fill keeps its
  original signature and body; the tail variant is a separate function because
  a `tail` parameter perturbed the inlined normal-call codegen
  (regvm_t_call_record 428 -> 441 instructions, regexp +2.1% cycles).
  Box2d checksum 546.1008391043414, test262 50/81192 threaded+record,
  differential corpus 400/0/0. Octane geomean cycles 0.9839 of the R1 build
  with no row worse than +0.34% (richards 0.9497, deltablue 0.9624,
  earleyboyer 0.9549).

